Wait, there's a bug: `ul.insertBefore(li, before ? before.nextElementSibling : null)` — when desired > cur, `before` = others[desired] is the first element to be pushed down; li needs to be inserted after it → before.nextElementSibling (in the current DOM order, including li) — hmm, in the current DOM, li is at index cur < desired. others[desired] is the element currently at the position li should occupy after insertion… let me re-derive.

Current DOM order: [... , X, li, Y, ...]. others = the list with li removed, in current order. desired = the index in others where li should be positioned after the move.

Case desired < cur: insert li before others[desired]. Example: cur=2, desired=1: DOM before move: A, B, li, C. others = [A, B, C]. desired=1 → insert li before others[1]=B. Result: A, li, B, C. ✓.

Case desired > cur: li moves down by (desired - cur); the elements at others[cur ... desired-1] move up by one position. In the current DOM, others[cur] is the first one right after li. li needs to be inserted after others[desired-1] (the element that should end up immediately before li). That's equivalent to ul.insertBefore(li, others[desired-1].nextElementSibling) — others[desired-1].nextElementSibling in the current DOM = the first element after it = …, in the current DOM, the elements of others occupy the positions excluding li. others[desired-1] is at some current position p ≥ cur+1. Its nextElementSibling is at p+1 (an element of others unless li is between them — but li is before all of these, so it isn't between them). So insertBefore(li, others[desired-1].nextElementSibling) places li right after others[desired-1].

Or, even simpler and unambiguous: compute the absolute target index = desired (in others space) = also the absolute index in the full list (because the full index of li = desired). Let's verify: full DOM length = others + 1. The desired full index = desired. So: `const ref = ul.children[desired]; ul.insertBefore(li, ref);` — if desired is less than li's current index, ul.children[desired] is the element currently at position desired (which should be pushed down). If desired is greater than the current index, ul.children[desired] is… example: DOM: A, B, li, C (cur=2, others=[A,B,C]), desired=3 → ref = ul.children[3] = C → insert before C → A, B, C, li. ✓. desired=1 → ref=ul.children[1]=B → A, li, B, C ✓. And when desired == children.length (== cur+?)? ul.children has 4 elements; desired=4 → ref = ul.children[4] = undefined → insertBefore(li, undefined) appends it.

